---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import HelpCard from '../components/HelpCard.astro';
import EmailBlock from '../components/EmailBlock.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<Layout title="331 Maple St — Prospect Lefferts Gardens">

<!-- ─── HERO ─── -->
<section class="hero">
  <Nav activePage="home" />

  <div class="hero-body">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span>Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn</span></p>
    <h1 class="hero-headline">
      What Gets Built Here<br /><em>Should Benefit PLG.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">
      The historic parish school at 335 Maple Street has been demolished. Our community is now calling for <strong>transparency, full legal compliance, meaningful engagement, and an enforceable affordable-housing commitment</strong> for the development that replaces it.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-ctas">
      <a href="#action" class="btn-primary">Help Shape What Comes Next</a>
      <button class="btn-share" id="hero-share">↗ Share This Page</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── UPDATE BANNER ─── -->
<section class="update-banner-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="update-banner reveal">
      <p class="update-tag">Update</p>
      <p>
        The parish school at 335 Maple Street, built in 1909, has been <strong>demolished</strong>, and construction of the new building is underway. Although the school can no longer be preserved, important questions remain about the development &mdash; its affordability, its compliance with zoning and permitting requirements, and the lack of meaningful community participation in the process.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── OUR ASKS ─── -->
<section class="about" id="asks">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-body reveal" style="max-width:760px;">
      <p class="section-label">What We're Asking For</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">A say in <em>what replaces it.</em></h2>
      <p>A development is coming to this site. Our goal is to make sure it is built openly, lawfully, and with a real benefit to the people who live around it.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="facts-list reveal" style="margin-top:2.5rem;">
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Accurate, current information</span> about the approved development plans, made available to the public.</div></li>
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">A full review of zoning and permitting</span> &mdash; FAR calculations, open-space ratios, and any air-rights transfers &mdash; confirmed by DOB and DCP.</div></li>
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Meaningful, enforceable affordable housing</span>, with terms in writing, rather than vague commitments.</div></li>
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Direct dialogue</span> among the church, the developer, elected officials, and the community.</div></li>
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">A tangible community benefit</span> from the development for the block and the neighborhood.</div></li>
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Contextual zoning for PLG</span>, so future development across the neighborhood is built to a predictable scale.</div></li>
      <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">A clear public record</span> of how the demolition and development process unfolded.</div></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="housing-position reveal">
      <p class="section-label" style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">Where We Stand on Housing</p>
      <p>We support the creation of new housing, including greater density where appropriate. We are asking that this development comply fully with applicable requirements, include meaningful affordable housing, and provide a clear public benefit to the community affected by it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── THE DEVELOPMENT ─── -->
<section class="about" id="development" style="background:var(--black); border-top:1px solid rgba(200,40,26,0.2); border-bottom:1px solid rgba(200,40,26,0.2);">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="before-after reveal">
      <div class="ba-card">
        <div class="ba-label ba-label-before">Demolished, 2026</div>
        <img src="/331mapleparish.png" alt="The former parish school at 335 Maple St, built 1909" />
        <p class="ba-caption">The 335 Maple St parish school, built 1909. Demolished in 2026 after community efforts to preserve it and obtain transparency around the redevelopment plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ba-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="ba-card">
        <div class="ba-label ba-label-after">Proposed</div>
        <img src="/331maplemockup.png" alt="Developer rendering of the proposed building at 331 Maple St" />
        <p class="ba-caption">Developer rendering of the proposed 12-story, 118-foot, 99-unit building. Affordability terms have not been made public.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="about-grid reveal" style="margin-top:4rem;">
      <div class="about-body reveal">
        <p class="section-label">The Development</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">What is proposed <em>for the site.</em></h2>
        <p>The land at 331&ndash;335 Maple Street is owned by the Roman Catholic Church of St. Francis of Assisi and St. Blaise. The parish entered into a long-term ground lease with developer <strong>Candor Capital</strong> for a new building on the site.</p>
        <p>In August 2025, plans were filed with the Department of Buildings for a <strong>12-story, 118-foot building with 99 residences</strong> and roughly 24,000 square feet of community-facility space, on a lot zoned R6. According to Father Vassalotti, church leadership was shown a six-story concept before the lease was signed; the plans filed with DOB are for twelve stories.</p>
        <p>In January 2026, demolition activity began at 335 Maple Street before permits had been issued. DOB issued a <strong>Stop Work Order</strong>, and the developer subsequently received violations and fines totaling roughly <strong>$30,000</strong>. The parish school was demolished in 2026, and construction of the new building is now underway.</p>
        <p>We are not opposed to new housing on this site. We are asking that the project comply fully with zoning and permitting law, that its affordability terms be made public and enforceable, and that it deliver a genuine benefit to the surrounding community.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <ul class="facts-list">
          <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">12 stories, 118 feet, 99 residences</span> on an R6 lot &mdash; roughly 98,000 sq ft total, including ~24,000 sq ft of community-facility space</div></li>
          <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Owner / applicant:</span> Candor Capital (Charles Wurzberger); land owned by the parish of St. Francis of Assisi and St. Blaise</div></li>
          <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Affordability terms not public</span> &mdash; no published, enforceable commitment on the number of affordable units or income bands</div></li>
          <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Stop Work Order in January 2026</span> after demolition activity began without a permit; ~$30,000 in violations and fines</div></li>
          <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">Open zoning question:</span> community members reviewing ACRIS have not identified a recorded air-rights transfer supporting the proposed floor area &mdash; DOB and DCP should confirm the FAR basis</div></li>
          <li><span class="fact-icon">⬤</span><div><span class="fact-strong">PLG has no contextual zoning</span> &mdash; unlike Park Slope, Windsor Terrace, and Kensington nearby</div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── WHAT HAPPENED ─── -->
<section class="urgency-section" id="timeline">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal" style="text-align:center; margin-bottom:3rem;">
      <p class="section-label">What Happened</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">A record of <em>the process.</em></h2>
      <p class="emails-intro" style="margin:0.5rem auto 0;">The 1909 parish school was demolished despite community efforts to preserve it and obtain greater transparency around the redevelopment plan. Its loss shows why residents need earlier notice, clearer public information, and a meaningful voice before irreversible decisions are made.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="urgency-grid urgency-grid--timeline-only">
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="timeline">
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Aug 2025 — Plans filed with DOB</strong><span>Applications for a 12-story, 118-foot, 99-unit building on the church-owned lot are filed. <a href="https://commercialobserver.com/2025/08/candor-capital-housing-331-maple-street-brooklyn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Commercial Observer</a>, <a href="https://brooklyneagle.com/articles/2025/08/22/brooklyn-developer-files-plans-for-99-apartments-on-church-owned-lot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brooklyn Eagle</a>, <a href="https://newyorkyimby.com/2025/09/permits-filed-for-331-maple-street-in-prospect-lefferts-gardens-brooklyn.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NY YIMBY</a></span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Sep 2025 — Community raises concerns</strong><span>Residents learn of the ground lease and the 12-story plan, and raise concerns at Community Board 9. Father Vassalotti tells neighbors the church was shown a six-story concept before signing.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Oct–Nov 2025 — Petition circulates</strong><span>Neighbors canvass PLG from Sterling to Clarkson Avenue. More than 600 residents sign a petition asking for a smaller building and contextual rezoning. Council Member Rita Joseph and Assemblymember Brian Cunningham engage.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Dec 2025 — Meeting with elected officials</strong><span>Council Member Joseph and Assemblymember Cunningham meet with residents and agree to examine the project's legal basis, including air rights and the terms of the lease. The Diocese is invited and declines to attend.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Jan 2026 — Stop Work Order</strong><span>Demolition activity begins at 335 Maple Street before permits are issued. DOB issues a Stop Work Order; the developer subsequently receives violations and fines totaling roughly $30,000. <a href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/buildings/index.page" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NYC DOB records</a></span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Feb 2026 — Meeting with a Diocese representative</strong><span>The Diocese agrees to a meeting with elected officials but, residents say, sends a representative without detailed knowledge of the development. Substantive questions about the lease and air rights go unanswered.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot">✓</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>Spring 2026 — Parish school demolished</strong><span>The 1909 parish school at 335 Maple Street is demolished.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="tl-dot" style="background:var(--accent); color:#000;">!</div>
            <div class="tl-text"><strong>2026 — Construction underway</strong><span>Construction of the new building has begun and has reached the second floor. The current approved plans, the affordability terms, the FAR basis, and any community-benefit commitment have not been made public. This site tracks those questions.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── BROADER CONTEXT ─── -->
<section class="about" id="context">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-body reveal" style="max-width:800px;">
      <p class="section-label">Broader Context</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">This isn't just about <em>one building.</em></h2>
      <p>PLG carries R6 zoning &mdash; a "Tower in the Park" framework from the 1960s with no height limit and a floor-area ratio of 2.43. Large lots and no contextual controls make it attractive to demolish smaller buildings and replace them with much larger ones, out of step with the surrounding streets.</p>
      <p>Prospect Lefferts Gardens has added thousands of units over the past decade. Many recent buildings are market-rate, with one-bedrooms renting for $3,000&ndash;$4,000 a month. The neighborhood's pressing need is for housing people who live here can actually afford &mdash; and for a predictable scale of development.</p>
      <p>Contextual zoning already exists in Park Slope, Windsor Terrace, Kensington, and parts of Bed-Stuy. PLG has been formally requesting a contextual rezoning study from City Planning since 2008. We are asking the City to take that step, so the next project on the next block is not decided the same way this one was.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── NEIGHBOR QUOTES ─── -->
<section class="quotes-strip">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="quotes-header reveal">
      <p class="section-label" style="color:rgba(245,239,224,0.4);">In Their Own Words</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Real neighbors. <em>Real stakes.</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="quotes-grid">
      <blockquote class="pull-quote reveal">
        <p>"The amount of development I've seen without consulting the local community has only resulted in 'affordable' apartments that locals can't afford, resulting in a money grab by developers while destroying the local neighborhood. We need to have a discussion about what our future can look like."</p>
        <footer><strong>Diana</strong><span>Midwood Street</span></footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="pull-quote reveal">
        <p>"We need all new housing built with contextual height restrictions to respect and preserve the existing neighborhoods. This proposed building violates the neighborhood context and would significantly change the nature of this neighborhood detrimentally."</p>
        <footer><strong>Margaret</strong><span>Midwood Street</span></footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="pull-quote reveal">
        <p>"We need affordable housing, not high-rises. Don't destroy the neighborhood."</p>
        <footer><strong>Benjamin</strong><span>Snyder Avenue</span></footer>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
    <div class="quotes-cta reveal">
      <a href="/voices" class="btn-outline">Read All Neighbor Voices →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── EMAIL TEMPLATES ─── -->
<section class="emails-section" id="emails">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="section-label">Email Templates</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Send a message. <em>Make it count.</em></h2>
      <p class="emails-intro">Personalized emails from residents carry real weight. Use these as a starting point &mdash; add your own name and your own connection to the neighborhood. Every template asks for transparency, legal compliance, and enforceable affordable housing, not for stopping construction.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion reveal">

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">Church</span> Diocese of Brooklyn &amp; Church Leadership</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-diocese-1" to="Bishop Robert Brennan" toEmail="bishopofbrooklyn@diobrook.org" subject="331 Maple St: Request for Transparency and an Affordable-Housing Commitment" body={`Dear Bishop Brennan,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens. I am writing about the development planned for the church-owned site at 331-335 Maple Street.

The parish school has been demolished, and a new building will be constructed. I am asking the Diocese to make sure that project is one the parish and the neighborhood can be proud of.

Specifically, I am asking the Diocese to:
- Make the current, approved development plans available to the community.
- Commit publicly to a defined amount of permanently affordable housing in the building, with the terms in writing.
- Meet with Council Member Rita Joseph, Assemblymember Brian Cunningham, and community representatives about the project and its impact on the block.

The Church has been a pillar of this neighborhood for generations. What is built on this site will carry the parish's name. I respectfully ask you to lead on transparency and affordability here.

Thank you for your time and consideration.

[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
          <EmailBlock id="email-diocese-2" to="Father Thomas Vassalotti, St. Francis of Assisi" toEmail="fathertomv@gmail.com" subject="A Message from Your PLG Neighbors: 331 Maple St" body={`Dear Father Tom,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens. I know you tried to advocate for a smaller building at 331 Maple Street, and I want to thank you for that.

The school is down now, and a new building is coming. I am writing to ask for your help with what happens next. Would you use your voice with the Diocese and the developer to press for:
- The current plans to be shared openly with the community.
- A written, enforceable commitment to affordable housing in the building.
- A meeting between Church leadership, the developer, our elected officials, and neighborhood representatives.

You know this block and the people on it. A building that includes real affordable housing and a genuine community benefit would be a good legacy for St. Francis of Assisi on Maple Street. We would be grateful for your help getting there.

Thank you,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
          <EmailBlock id="email-diocese-3" to="Vincent LeVien, Director of External Affairs, Diocese of Brooklyn" toEmail="vlevien@desalesmedia.org" subject="Request: Diocese Engagement on the 331 Maple St Development" body={`Dear Mr. LeVien,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens writing to ask the Diocese of Brooklyn to engage with our community on the development at 331-335 Maple Street.

The parish school has been demolished and a new building is planned. Council Member Rita Joseph and Assemblymember Brian Cunningham have been seeking a meeting with Church leadership about the project for months.

I am asking you to help arrange that meeting, and to encourage the Diocese to:
- Share the current, approved development plans with the community.
- Commit in writing to a defined amount of affordable housing in the building.

Residents are not asking to stop construction. We are asking for transparency and for the affordable housing this neighborhood needs. Please bring this request to Bishop Brennan.

Thank you,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">City Hall</span> Mayor's Community Affairs Unit</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-mayor" to="constituentservices@cau.nyc.gov | Mayor's Community Affairs Unit" toEmail="constituentservices@cau.nyc.gov" subject="Community Concern: Transparency and Affordability at 331 Maple St, Prospect Lefferts Gardens" body={`Hello,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens writing to the Mayor's Community Affairs Unit about the development at 331-335 Maple Street.

The 1909 parish school on this church-owned site has been demolished, and a 12-story, 99-unit building is planned by developer Candor Capital. The current plans, the affordability terms, and the zoning basis for the project have not been made public.

I am asking the Mayor's office to:
- Help convene the Diocese, the developer, and our elected officials for a meeting with community representatives.
- Ask DOB and City Planning to confirm the project's compliance with FAR, open-space, and air-rights requirements, and to publish that review.
- Encourage a binding affordable-housing commitment and a clear community benefit as part of the project.

Thank you,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">City Council</span> Council Member Rita Joseph</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-rita" to="rjoseph@council.nyc.gov | CC: BGrady@council.nyc.gov" toEmail="rjoseph@council.nyc.gov" subject="331 Maple St: Please Obtain and Publish the Current Plans, and Push for Affordability" body={`Dear Council Member Joseph,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens. Thank you for the work your office has already done on 331 Maple Street.

Now that the parish school has been demolished and a new building is planned, I am asking your office to:
- Obtain the developer's current, approved plans and make them public.
- Press the Diocese and the developer for a written, enforceable affordable-housing commitment in the building.
- Ask what community benefit the project will provide, and advocate for one that matters on this block &mdash; for example, support for Providence House on Lincoln Road.
- Continue pursuing a contextual rezoning study for PLG with City Planning.

The community appreciates your persistence and wants to support this effort however we can.

Respectfully,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">State Assembly</span> Assemblymember Brian Cunningham</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-cunningham" to="cunninghamba@nyassembly.gov" toEmail="cunninghamba@nyassembly.gov" subject="331 Maple St: State-Level Support for Transparency and Affordable Housing" body={`Dear Assemblymember Cunningham,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens. Thank you for pressing the Diocese of Brooklyn for a meeting alongside Council Member Joseph.

With the parish school now demolished and a 12-story building planned at 331-335 Maple Street, I am asking your office to:
- Keep pushing for a meeting with Church leadership and the developer.
- Use state housing programs and leverage to encourage a real, enforceable affordable-housing commitment in the building.
- Support a formal review by DOB and City Planning of the project's FAR calculations and any air-rights transfers, with the results made public.

Residents support new housing here. We are asking that it be affordable, lawful, and open to public scrutiny.

Respectfully,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">City Agency</span> NYC Department of Buildings</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-dob" to="buildings@buildings.nyc.gov | CC: brooklyn@buildings.nyc.gov" toEmail="buildings@buildings.nyc.gov" subject="Request for Review: Zoning and FAR Basis at 331-335 Maple St, Brooklyn 11225" body={`Hello,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens writing about the development at 331-335 Maple Street, Brooklyn, NY 11225.

The site is zoned R6. Plans on file are for a 12-story, 118-foot, 99-unit building. Community members who have reviewed ACRIS have not identified a recorded zoning-lot-development agreement or air-rights transfer that would support the proposed floor area.

I am requesting that DOB:
- Confirm the FAR calculation, open-space ratio, and lot-area basis for the approved plans.
- Confirm whether any air-rights transfer between the development lot and the larger church lot has been recorded and relied upon.
- Make the results of that review publicly available.

I would also ask that the January 2026 Stop Work Order and the associated violations at this address remain fully resolved and on the public record.

Thank you,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">City Planning</span> DCP Director Sideya Sherman</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-dcp" to="ssherman@planning.nyc.gov | CC: BK_DL@planning.nyc.gov" toEmail="ssherman@planning.nyc.gov" subject="331 Maple St Review and a Contextual Rezoning Study for Prospect Lefferts Gardens" body={`Dear Director Sherman,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens writing about two related matters.

First, the development at 331-335 Maple Street: a 12-story, 118-foot, 99-unit building is planned on an R6 lot. I am asking City Planning to review the FAR calculations, open-space ratios, and any air-rights transfers for the approved plans, and to publish that review.

Second, contextual zoning: PLG is one of the last neighborhoods bordering Prospect Park without contextual protections. Park Slope, Windsor Terrace, and Kensington have height limits; PLG does not. The neighborhood's zoning was last updated in 1961, and the community has been requesting a contextual rezoning study since 2008. I am asking you to begin that study, so future development is built to a predictable scale.

Thank you,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="accordion-item">
        <button class="accordion-trigger">
          <span class="acc-label"><span class="acc-tag">Local</span> Public Advocate</span>
          <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="accordion-content">
          <EmailBlock id="email-advocate" to="gethelp@advocate.nyc.gov | NYC Public Advocate" toEmail="gethelp@advocate.nyc.gov" subject="Request: Transparency and Affordability at 331 Maple St, Prospect Lefferts Gardens" body={`Hello,

My name is [YOUR NAME], and I am a resident of Prospect Lefferts Gardens writing to ask for the Public Advocate's help on the development at 331-335 Maple Street.

The 1909 parish school on this church-owned site has been demolished, and a 12-story, 99-unit building is planned by developer Candor Capital. Demolition activity began without a permit in January 2026 and drew a Stop Work Order and roughly $30,000 in fines. The current plans and the affordability terms have not been made public, and the Diocese has not met substantively with elected officials about the project.

I am asking the Public Advocate's office to:
- Publicly urge the Diocese of Brooklyn to meet with Council Member Joseph and Assemblymember Cunningham and to release the current plans.
- Call for a binding affordable-housing commitment and a defined community benefit in the project.
- Support a DOB and City Planning review of the project's FAR and air-rights basis, with the findings made public.

Thank you,
[YOUR NAME]
Prospect Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn`} />
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── CENTRAL MESSAGE ─── -->
<section class="creed-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="creed reveal">
      <p>The school is gone, but the community still deserves a say in what replaces it. We are advocating for a <em>transparent, legally compliant development process</em>, <em>meaningful affordable housing</em>, and a <em>real benefit</em> for Prospect Lefferts Gardens.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── TAKE ACTION ─── -->
<section class="help-section" id="action">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="section-label">Take Action</p>
